{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"auspicious-torrid-curlew","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem / opportunity\n\nThe source process audit found 49.0% first-pass plan review and a p90 of eight attempts. That is meaningful churn, but the project already has a mature deterministic pre-LLM floor: current code implements P1-P11. A proposal framed as \"add deterministic preflight\" would duplicate existing architecture and risk promoting noisy heuristics.\n\n## Prior decisions that constrain this idea\n\nelfin-floor-gouge established the deterministic tier as the default blocking layer and semantic LLM review as the architecture/context layer. depleted-lethal-brownbear promoted P10/P11 only after pass/block backtests demonstrated discrimination and low false-positive rates; it rejected spec-by-example and riskiest-assumption blockers because they did not discriminate. childsafe-bizarre-lorikeet found that criterion-skipping on revised plans is unsafe: 37% of 894 revisions blocked on a criterion absent from the first block. It separately identified identical-material re-reviews as a quality-neutral optimization target.\n\nThis idea must extend the existing floor rather than build a parallel preflight or weaken semantic review.\n\n## Outcome hypothesis\n\nPersisted plan-review and completion sidecars can reveal a small Pareto set of recurrent, mechanically decidable failure classes. Replay-tested candidates may reduce avoidable LLM churn without increasing false blocks or hiding cross-cutting findings.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Candidate checks must be mechanically decidable from evidence available before the LLM review.\n- Unsupported or uncertain grounding fails open and records coverage.\n- No criterion-skipping based only on unchanged-looking plan sections.\n- No promotion directly from intuition to blocking; use offline replay and an advisory observation period.\n- Preserve semantic review for architecture, failure modes, portability, testing strategy, and tradeoffs.\n- Optimize total review quality/cost, not PASS rate in isolation.\n\n## Questions before promotion\n\n- What is the Pareto distribution of blocking findings by normalized failure class and remediation?\n- Which classes can be decided deterministically with pre-review material?\n- What pass/block cohorts and sampling method establish a false-positive ceiling?\n- What precision/recall and minimum support thresholds justify advisory rollout and later blocking?\n- How will abstention, unsupported stacks, and stale grounding be represented?\n- Which identical-material rerun causes can be eliminated without changing review coverage?\n- What guard metrics ensure completion quality and defect signals do not worsen?\n\n## Evidence\n\nDiscovered from unpleasing-hairy-yellowlegs; source audit bleakish-stational-cob. Prior work: elfin-floor-gouge, depleted-lethal-brownbear, papyral-scarlet-malamute, childsafe-bizarre-lorikeet, big-mammalian-bronco.","id":"fb2e-2aa1-9e0f-457a","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["process","planning","quality"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Mine review history for the next low-false-positive deterministic plan checks"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786825415083307001,"uuid":"3fc62143-ebdf-470f-928e-0f4c76ea6d07"}